  "id": "video_edit_bernini_r_video_editing_ref_autoprompt",
  "name": "Wan 2.2 Bernini-R — video editing with reference (auto-prompt)",
  "title": "Move a video into a picture",
  "summary": "Give it a clip of someone moving and a picture of a character in a place; an assistant writes the instructions for you and that character, in that place, follows the clip's movement loosely — the big gestures, not the exact steps. No prompt to write — but one of the slowest cards.",
  "description": "Restyle a video using a reference image, with the edit prompt written for you. Upload a clip and a reference image (a character, look, or setting to bring in) — a Gemma-4 vision model describes the video and the reference, then writes the Bernini-R edit instruction automatically. The result follows the general motion of your video (the big gestures, not step for step) while importing the character and setting from the reference image.\n\n• **No prompt to type** — the instruction is generated from your two inputs. To write the instruction yourself, use the plain \"video editing with a reference image\" version instead.\n• **Edit type**: pick how the reference is used.\n• **Fast mode** (Turbo LoRA) is on by default — ~6 steps. Turn it off for slower, higher-quality 40-step runs.\n* Keep resolution of input image and video low : model train on 832 x 832 px / 832 x 480 px / 480 x 832 px.\n\nHeavy (dual 14B Wan models + a vision model) — keep clips short.",
  "category": "video-edit",
